paragraphs:
When he saw it, he ran toward the tree.
Its fruit seemed to be diamonds (?);
its branches hung down, lovely to see.
Its limbs bore crystal (antimony?);
fruit covered it and dazzled the hero's eyes.

While homes are built and tablets sealed,
while brothers remain at odds,
while hatred and quarrels endure in the country,
while rivers continue carrying their waters (to the sea?), and so on,
no human being can foresee death's shape or timing.
When the alu and eziz demons greet a man,
the great Anunnaki [come together],
and the goddess who assigns each fate joins their council.
Together they settle who lives and who dies,
yet humankind cannot know when its last day will arrive.

"He no longer kisses the wife he loved,
or strikes the wife he hated.
He no longer kisses the son he loved,
or strikes the son he hated.
The miseries of the underworld have taken him;
dark mother Nin-azu, dark and darker,
has taken him too.
Her bright white body is not covered by clothes;
her breast, like the lid of a box, [is not * * *].
Eabani has [gone down?] from earth into [the dark?].
Pestilence did not take him, nor consumption;
the earth itself took him.
Nergal's hidden, merciless demon did not take him;
the earth took him.
The battlefield did not kill him;
the earth took him."
